WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
When it comes to building modern user interfaces,

2
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.320
Jetpack Compose and React Native approach rendering

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.040
with entirely different core architectures.

4
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.280
In Compose, the compiler tracks which composables read state.

5
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.000
Only dependent nodes update when state changes.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.320
The rest are bypassed automatically.

7
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.560
React is the exact opposite.

8
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.480
By default, a parent's update sends a render signal cascading down,

9
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.120
hitting every single connected child node.

10
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.920
This exhaustive cascade executes regardless

11
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.960
of whether the child components actual data changed.

12
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.320
A node with completely static text will rebuild itself

13
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.360
simply because the parent above it triggered a state change.

14
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.480
To achieve the native level efficiency seen in Compose,

15
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.680
engineers building in React have to manually intervene.

16
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.720
You must explicitly opt in to smart rendering.

17
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.560
React provides three distinct tools to interrupt this default behavior.

18
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.240
First is the memo wrapper.

19
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.600
Second is the use memo hook, and finally, call back.

20
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.200
Without a deliberate strategy to orchestrate these three hooks,

21
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.880
an application burns through CPU cycles unnecessarily,

22
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.400
causing the interface to stutter during complex interactions.

23
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.480
Consider a standard React tree.

24
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.360
We have a parent node connected to an expensive child

25
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.720
and a static child.

26
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.480
When the parent state updates,

27
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.680
that red render signal travels down both paths.

28
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.840
The first line of defense is memo.

29
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.920
React's manual skip function.

30
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.760
When you wrap a component in memo,

31
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.840
you place a logic gate in front of it.

32
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.440
As the render signal approaches,

33
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.640
memo intercepts it to run a shallow equality check.

34
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.720
This check compares the incoming properties

35
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.920
against the exact properties from the previous render cycle.

36
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.960
If React determines the props are mathematically identical,

37
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.320
the logic gate holds.

38
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.040
The render cascade is aborted for that specific branch,

39
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.800
saving CPU cycles.

40
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.280
While memo acts as a highly effective shield,

41
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.320
its utility depends on the stability of incoming data.

42
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.920
Its crops change unnecessarily, the shield breaks.

43
00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.160
If the expensive child component contains a heavy computational loop,

44
00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.320
like sorting a massive data array,

45
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.520
it forces the main thread to freeze while processing.

46
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.640
Use memo caches the result of these heavy computations,

47
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.840
holding the processed data in memory between render cycles,

48
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.760
so the math isn't repeated.

49
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.680
This diagram illustrates a balancing scale of performance costs.

50
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.160
The first rule of use memo is that the computation

51
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.000
must be visibly slow, profiling at over one millisecond.

52
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.880
At that weight, the CPU cost clearly outweighs

53
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.040
the cache cost of allocating memory.

54
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.080
Structurally, deploy use memo when the output is passed

55
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.400
directly into a memo-wrapped child.

56
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.640
But watch what happens when we swap the heavy loop

57
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.040
for basic math or string concatenation.

58
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.040
The scale instantly tips.

59
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.680
Memoization requires its own memory allocation

60
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.320
and CPU cycles for dependency comparisons.

61
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.200
For simple operations, caching takes longer

62
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.520
than just recalculating the value.

63
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.920
Use memo is a specialized tool,

64
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.960
applying it indiscriminately to grades performance

65
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.120
by stacking up structural overhead

66
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.320
that eclipses the actual computational savings.

67
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.080
Even with memo in place, the shield has a vulnerability.

68
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.000
The shallow equality check fails

69
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.720
when it encounters referential instability.

70
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.160
When apparent renders, it executes its internal code,

71
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.000
recreating functions from scratch

72
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.000
with a new memory address pointer.

73
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.560
When passed down, the shallow equality check

74
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.520
sees the pointer has changed, the barrier shatters,

75
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.520
and the child re-renders anyway.

76
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.720
To prevent this, developers must use use callback

77
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.480
to lock the function's memory reference

78
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.120
in place across multiple render cycles.

79
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.400
Under the hood, use callback caches the function definition

80
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.360
itself.

81
00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.120
By wrapping the function, the pointer

82
00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.400
locks statically.

83
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.000
The next time the parent updates,

84
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.640
it passes down the exact same memory address.

85
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.520
The child's memo shield recognizes the identical reference

86
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:48.880
and holds firm.

87
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.920
Use callback does not make the function execute faster.

88
00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.120
Its only architectural purpose is to maintain a stable reference

89
00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.400
pointer to protect downstream shallow equality checks.

90
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.920
Both use memo and use callback rely on one control mechanism,

91
00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.440
the dependency array, which dictates when the cache resets.

92
00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.360
Misconfiguring this array is the leading cause

93
00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.280
of memoization errors and react.

94
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.680
The first failure mode is the stale closure.

95
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.720
This occurs when developers omit required variables

96
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.280
from the array, with missing dependencies,

97
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.240
the cached function executes using outdated variables

98
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.760
trapped in its original closure, causing silent desinks.

99
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.720
The inverse error is overspecifying dependencies,

100
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.360
which happens when engineers pack the array with variables

101
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.880
that change constantly.

102
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.000
When the cache invalidates and resets on every single cycle,

103
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.320
you defeat memoization entirely.

104
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.960
You pay the checks performance cost and still recalculate

105
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.080
the value.

106
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.160
Constructing a correct dependency array

107
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.160
requires exact precision.

108
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.320
You must track exactly what the function needs

109
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.080
to remain mathematically accurate and absolutely nothing more.

110
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.800
Theoretical mechanics only matter if you can verify them

111
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.320
in a live environment.

112
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.520
To diagnose rendering cascades, we have to rely on objective

113
00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.400
measurement.

114
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.200
This brings us to the react native DevTools profiler,

115
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.920
accessible by pressing J in the metroterminal.

116
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.480
It serves as the definitive source of truth

117
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.240
for component performance.

118
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.720
The profiling workflow is straightforward.

119
00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.800
Start a recording in the DevTools,

120
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.080
manually trigger the heavy interaction on the physical device,

121
00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.440
and stop the recording to generate a telemetry report.

122
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.040
This flame chart shows where the processor spent its time.

123
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.560
Clicking a prominent component bar opens a panel asking,

124
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.080
why did this render?

125
00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.160
The DevTools categorize the trigger,

126
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.600
whether state updated, a prop changed, or crucially,

127
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.200
if the component updated simply because its parent re-rendered.

128
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.480
That diagnostic data drives a simple decision tree.

129
00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.440
If a parent is forcing a static child to update,

130
00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.280
deploy memo as a shield.

131
00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.920
If a heavy calculation exceeds one millisecond,

132
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.520
cache it with use memo.

133
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.080
And if a changing memory address breaks that shield,

134
00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.320
stabilize it with use callback.

135
00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.600
Mastering these three tools takes react from an exhaustive

136
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.840
brute force architecture to a surgically precise rendering engine.

137
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.640
Once you have the render cycles under control,

138
00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.040
the application interaction is fast,

139
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.240
but there is one final bottleneck left to solve.

140
00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.000
Start up latency.

141
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.280
Getting your app to launch immediately on a cold start

142
00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.720
requires analyzing Hermi's bytecode manipulation,

143
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.120
executing lazy loading strategies,

144
00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.400
and rigorously auditing the final bundle size.

145
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.400
That is where we go next.

